      "hints": [
        "Expand $R\\cos(2x + \\alpha) = R\\cos(2x)\\cos\\alpha - R\\sin(2x)\\sin\\alpha$ and equate coefficients.",
        "Calculate $R = \\sqrt{3^2 + (\\sqrt{3})^2}$ and $\\tan\\alpha = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ to find exact values."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Expand the $R$-formula using the compound angle identity:\n$$R\\cos(2x + \\alpha) = R\\cos(2x)\\cos\\alpha - R\\sin(2x)\\sin\\alpha$$",
        "Equate coefficients with $3\\cos(2x) - \\sqrt{3}\\sin(2x)$:\n$$R\\cos\\alpha = 3$$\n$$R\\sin\\alpha = \\sqrt{3}$$",
        "Calculate $R$ by squaring and adding:\n$$R^2 = (R\\cos\\alpha)^2 + (R\\sin\\alpha)^2 = 3^2 + (\\sqrt{3})^2 = 9 + 3 = 12$$\n$$R = \\sqrt{12} = 2\\sqrt{3}$$",
        "Calculate $\\alpha$ by dividing the two equations:\n$$\\tan\\alpha = \\dfrac{R\\sin\\alpha}{R\\cos\\alpha} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$$\nSince $0 < \\alpha < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$:\n$$\\alpha = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$$",
        "State the expression in $R$-form:\n$$2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(2x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$$\nwith $R = 2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\alpha = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$."
      ],
      "checking": {
